      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.2 Method for Testing",
      "text": "17.3.2 Method for Testing\nA30 Metre Ride-By Test.\nThe sound levels will be measured with the sound meter/microphone fixed on a tripod, in \nthe horizontal position, 30 metres from the edge of the track at a high speed point.\nB2 Metre Max Method \nRefer to Appendix C: 17.3.6 Use of Sound Level Meters: 2 Metre Max Method.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.3 Sound Testing",
      "text": "17.3.3 Sound Testing",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.3.1 Sound testing must be carried out at all permitted Events however it is not mandatory to test all",
      "text": "17.3.3.1 Sound testing must be carried out at all permitted Events however it is not mandatory to test all \nMachines.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.3.2 Where government regulations or planning orders exist in relation to lower sound emissions or",
      "text": "17.3.3.2 Where government regulations or planning orders exist in relation to lower sound emissions or \nwhere a Venue has lower sound emission requirements as part of the hire contract, the sound \nemission required will prevail over Appendix C.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.4 Sound Control During Competition",
      "text": "17.3.4 Sound Control During Competition",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.4.1 The Sound Control Officer (SCO) must arrive in sufficient time for discussions with the Clerk of",
      "text": "17.3.4.1 The Sound Control Officer (SCO) must arrive in sufficient time for discussions with the Clerk of \nthe Course and other technical officials in order that a suitable test site and testing policy can be \nagreed.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.4.2 Machines can be tested before, or after competing in an Event, chosen by ballot, or as required",
      "text": "17.3.4.2 Machines can be tested before, or after competing in an Event, chosen by ballot, or as required \nby a Steward, Clerk of Course or SCO.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.4.3 Sound testing apparatus must:",
      "text": "17.3.4.3 Sound testing apparatus must:\na)    Comply with International standard IEC 651, Type 1 or Type 2.\nb)    Include a compatible calibrator, which must be used immediately before testing begins and \nalways just prior to a re-test if a disciplinary sanction may be imposed.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.4.4 Sound testing apparatus must be set to:",
      "text": "17.3.4.4 Sound testing apparatus must be set to:\na)    ‘Fast response’,\nb)    ‘A’ weighted,\nc)    Select range High 80~130 dB,\nd)    Activate the function MAX MIN - set on MAX.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5 Machine Testing",
      "text": "17.3.5 Machine Testing",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5.1 No person may compete in any Event on a Machine whose sound emissions exceed the",
      "text": "17.3.5.1 No person may compete in any Event on a Machine whose sound emissions exceed the \nprescribed levels.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5.2 If a Machine fails, it can be represented for re-testing.",
      "text": "17.3.5.2 If a Machine fails, it can be represented for re-testing.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5.3 A Machine which does not comply with the sound limits can be presented several times.",
      "text": "17.3.5.3 A Machine which does not comply with the sound limits can be presented several times.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5.5 Provided sound emission levels are not exceeded, exhaust systems may operate without",
      "text": "17.3.5.5 Provided sound emission levels are not exceeded, exhaust systems may operate without \nsilencers.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.5.6 Tests shall not take place in the rain.",
      "text": "17.3.5.6 Tests shall not take place in the rain.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.6 Use of Sound Level Meters (2 Meter Max Method)",
      "text": "17.3.6 Use of Sound Level Meters (2 Meter Max Method)",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.6.1 Set up of the sound meter and the Machine:",
      "text": "17.3.6.1 Set up of the sound meter and the Machine:\na)    The sound levels will be measured with the sound meter/microphone fixed on a tripod, in \nthe horizontal position, at the rear of the Machine,\nb)    For the place and position of the Machine, ensure that there are no solid obstacles within a \n10 metre radius of the microphone,\nc)    The sound meter will be positioned at a distance of two metres behind the Machine, with an \nangle of 45° away from the centerline, on the exhaust side and at a height of 1.35 metres \nabove the ground, with the sound meter level,\nd)    The two metre distance is measured from the point where the centre of rear tyre touches \nthe ground,\ne)    It is preferred that the tests are conducted on soft ground, to prevent reverberation, for \nexample, grass or fine gravel,\nf)    In other than moderate wind, Machines should face forward into the wind direction,\ng)    The ambient sound level must remain lower than 100 dB/A.\n\n269\nAPP C: SOUND EMISSION AND FUEL\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.6.2 Positioning of the Machine:",
      "text": "17.3.6.2 Positioning of the Machine: \n The reference points:\na)    For a Machine: the contact point of the rear wheel on the ground,\nb)    For Machines fitted with two exhaust outputs, the measurement will be made on the side of \nthe air intake. If a central positioned air intake is used, both sides will be tested,\nc)    For Sidecars: the contact point of the side wheel on the ground.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.6.3 Testing method:",
      "text": "17.3.6.3 Testing method:\na)    The measurement can be made with the Machine on its wheels or on a stand, with a hot \nengine,\nb)    During a sound test, Machines not equipped with a gear box neutral must be placed on a \nstand,\nc)    The SCO should stand beside the Machine, opposite the microphone and not screen or \nstand between the Machine and the microphone. An assistant, placed on the left side of the \nMachine, shall disengage the clutch,\nd)    The SCO shall open the throttle as fast as possible until full open throttle (instantly, within \n0.3 seconds) and keep at max engine ‘RPM’ for at least one second. To end, the SCO will \nrelease the throttle quickly,\ne)    If the result exceeds the limit, including ‘after fire’, the SCO shall test the Machine a \nmaximum of two more times,\nf)    For Machines equipped with an engine RPM limiter, opening the throttle will be made - \ninstantly, within 0.3 seconds - and kept open until at least one second has evolved and/or \nwhen there is an audible sign of over revving the engine,\ng)    For Machines without an engine ‘RPM’ limiter, the opening of the throttle will have to be \nlower than two seconds and/or when there is an audible sign of over-revving the engine,\nh)    If the engine tends to suffocate, close the throttle slightly and re-open the throttle,\ni)    If detonations appear, the measurement must be started again,\nj)    The numbers obtained from the test shall not be rounded down,\nk)    For the sound level measurement, the handling of the throttle is limited only to the SCO, \nwho shall open the throttle themselves in order to minimize the influence by another \noperator (for that, it is helpful to have the microphone equipped with an extension cable to \nthe sound meter),\n\n270\nAPP C: SOUND EMISSIONS AND FUEL\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT\nl)    The Machine may be tested in gear,\nm)    Exhaust system damage during the Race: silencers fitted with removable end cap/adapters/\ninserts to reduce sound must be securely mounted. If removable end cap/adapters/inserts \nbecome separated from the silencer during practice, qualifying or Race and the Machine \nexceeds posted sound limits, the silencer will be deemed modified and a penalty will apply. \nMachine exhaust systems sustaining damage during the Race must meet post-Race sound \nlimits,\nn)    Any competitor whose Machine is tested post-Race and found to exceed the maximum \nallowed post-Race sound limit will be relegated three finishing positions for the previous \nRace. Post-qualifying, the rider’s best lap will be removed from the results,\no)    Machines found to be using lower RPM limits for the static sound test than what is used on \ntrack will be immediately Excluded from the previous Race. This may be verified by the use \nof a tachometer. For Machines fitted with map select switches, all switch positions may be \ntested,\np)    Machines that do not comply with sound test limits pre-Race will not be permitted to enter \nthe course.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.6.4 Post-Race testing protocol:",
      "text": "17.3.6.4 Post-Race testing protocol: \na)    Immediately after each Race, Machines selected for sound testing must proceed directly to \nthe sound testing area, \nb)    Only two crew members per Machine, including the rider, are permitted in the sound testing \narea at any one time, \nc)    Where a competitor’s Machine does not proceed directly to the sound testing area, the \ncompetitor will be subject to a three position relegation, \nd)    Machines selected for post-Race sound testing must not be adjusted or interfered with until \nsound test is completed. A three position relegation will apply for non-compliance.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.7 Fuel",
      "text": "17.3.7          Fuel\nAMust be unleaded, fuel available on demand from a bowser at five separate service \nstations in any five Australian states or territories and\ni) No more than 100 RON,\nii) Contain no additives other than those added at the point of manufacture except for \nlubricating oils for 2-stroke Machines,\niii) Be readily available in Australia, and\niv) Comply with the “Fuel Quality Standards Act 2000”.\nBe a brand of fuel homologated by MA.\nBLevel A Fuel, or\nPure methanol.\nCLevel A Fuel, or\nHomologated substitutes for leaded fuel (substitutes can be found on the MA website).\nDLevel A Fuel,\nPure methanol, or \nHomologated substitutes for leaded fuel (substitutes can be found on the MA website).",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.8 Fuel Exceptions",
      "text": "17.3.8 Fuel Exceptions\nAMoto2 and Moto3 GP Machines may use fuel in accordance with the FIM Technical \nRegulations.\nBPure methanol.\nCHomologated substitutes for leaded fuel (substitutes can be found on the MA website).\nDUpper cylinder lubricant in 4-stroke engines.\n\n271\nAPP C: SOUND EMISSION AND FUEL\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.9 Fuel Warning",
      "text": "17.3.9 Fuel Warning",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.9.1 Fuels and lubricants are highly specialised substances and participants must be aware they",
      "text": "17.3.9.1 Fuels and lubricants are highly specialised substances and participants must be aware they \nmay contain substances which are extremely dangerous to human health if misused, inhaled or \nallowed to contact skin.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.9.2 Some of the components of fuel and lubricants are suspected of having the potential to cause",
      "text": "17.3.9.2 Some of the components of fuel and lubricants are suspected of having the potential to cause \ncancer in rare circumstances.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.9.3 The use of petrol as a general cleaning and washing agent is a common misuse of a potentially",
      "text": "17.3.9.3 The use of petrol as a general cleaning and washing agent is a common misuse of a potentially \ndangerous substance.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.9.4 Fuels should be used and stored with extreme care and in accordance with the manufacturer’s",
      "text": "17.3.9.4 Fuels should be used and stored with extreme care and in accordance with the manufacturer’s \ninstructions.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10 Fuel Testing",
      "text": "17.3.10 Fuel Testing",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10.1 For any Event, meeting or series, the RCB may direct that no fuels other than fuels of prescribed",
      "text": "17.3.10.1 For any Event, meeting or series, the RCB may direct that no fuels other than fuels of prescribed \nspecifications and from a prescribed source may be used.\na)    Tests to ensure that only prescribed fuels are used in an Event, meeting or series, may be \nadministered at any time and place during the course of the same,\nb)    The Clerk of Course, Race Director or Chief Technical Officer may direct the administration \nof fuel tests.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10.2 Fuel tests must comply with the following procedures:",
      "text": "17.3.10.2 Fuel tests must comply with the following procedures:\na)    All containers for holding samples must be clean and constructed of robust non-reactive \nimpermeable material, must be sealable, and must have provision for identification,\nb)    Equipment used for the extraction of fuel from Machines must be clean and constructed of \nfuel non-reactive material,\nc)    Fuel testing must be conducted by an MA approved fuel screening company using an \ninfrared fuel analysis spectrometer.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10.3 Where onsite fuel testing is provided by the Promoter or RCB, and is conducted at an MA",
      "text": "17.3.10.3 Where onsite fuel testing is provided by the Promoter or RCB, and is conducted at an MA \npermitted Event by an MA approved fuel screening company using an infrared fuel analysis \nspectrometer, the following will apply:\na)    Competitors’ Machines being tested in this manner must remain in the parc fermé/impound \narea under the control of technical officials until the result of the test is known,\nb)    Where a sample has been taken from a competitor’s Machine and the sample of fuel is \noutside the prescribed specifications as contained within the GCRs or SRs, this is referred \nto as a positive result,\nc)    Where a positive sample has been taken from a competitor’s Machine, a second sample \nmust be taken and tested to confirm the initial positive result,\nd)    Penalties and/or sanctions listed in these GCRs can be applied based on a positive result of \nthe second sample taken,\ne)    If a competitor wishes to protest a positive result of the onsite test, the secondary testing \nprocedure will apply, and\ni)     The Machine must not have left the confines of the parc fermé/impound area and still \nbe under control of the technical officials,\nii)    If the Machine is removed from the parc fermé/impound area before the secondary \ntesting procedure is implemented, the secondary testing procedure cannot take place \nand consequently, the results from the original test sample will stand and all penalties \nwill apply.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10.4 Where fuel testing is required and an onsite fuel testing service is not provided by the Promoter or",
      "text": "17.3.10.4 Where fuel testing is required and an onsite fuel testing service is not provided by the Promoter or \nRCB, the secondary testing procedure may be applied.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.10.5 Secondary testing procedure:",
      "text": "17.3.10.5 Secondary testing procedure:\na)    Two samples (sample A and sample B) of not less than 250ml each or where the quantity of \nfuel available in the Machine is less than 2 x 250ml, then as much fuel as is available will be \ntaken and divided evenly between the two samples. These samples must be taken while the \nMachine is still in the parc fermé/impound area and must be placed in separate containers,\nb)    Once samples are placed in containers, the containers must immediately be sealed and \nidentified by reference to the Machine from which the sample was taken. This information \nmust be entered on a fuel sample certificate which must certify the date, place and time of \ntaking the sample, the identity of the Machine from which the sample was taken and the \nidentity of the rider,\n\n272\nAPP C: SOUND EMISSIONS AND FUEL\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT\nc)    Both samples must remain in the control of the official who administered the test,\nd)    The rider or the representative must sign the fuel sample certificate acknowledging samples \nhave been taken and are sealed,\ne)    All samples held by the official must be delivered to the RCB as soon as practicable after \nthe Event. The RCB must deliver sample A and B as soon as practicable to a laboratory \napproved by MA, where sample A must be tested for content and quality in accordance with \nstandard scientific procedures under the Fuel Quality Standards Act 2000,\nf)    The RCB must, as soon as practicable after receipt of the results, notify the rider or rider’s \nteam representative and MA,\ng)    If the rider is dissatisfied with the test result of sample A, they may, at the rider’s cost, \nrequest sample B be tested at an MA approved laboratory in their presence.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.11 Sanctions",
      "text": "17.3.11        Sanctions",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.11.1 Where a competitor has received a positive fuel testing result and has been found to have used a",
      "text": "17.3.11.1 Where a competitor has received a positive fuel testing result and has been found to have used a \nfuel which is outside of the prescribed specifications, the following sanctions will apply:\na)    The competitor will be Excluded from the results of the Event where the samples were \nobtained, and Excluded from all Events Competition the competitor participated in prior to \nthe sampling at that Event,\nb)    All costs associated with an individual competitor’s laboratory testing of fuel samples will be \nborne by the competitor,\nc)    Fines in addition to any other sanctions may also be applied for this breach of the GCRs.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.11.2 The laboratory tests are considered to be “judge of fact” and the penalties, as applied, are",
      "text": "17.3.11.2 The laboratory tests are considered to be “judge of fact” and the penalties, as applied, are \nmandatory and not open to protest or appeal.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.11.3 If the outcome of the laboratory test returns a negative result, the cost of the secondary testing",
      "text": "17.3.11.3 If the outcome of the laboratory test returns a negative result, the cost of the secondary testing \nwill be borne by the Promoter or RCB.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.12 Refuelling",
      "text": "17.3.12        Refuelling",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.12.1 During refuelling, each Machine must be stationary with the engine stopped.",
      "text": "17.3.12.1 During refuelling, each Machine must be stationary with the engine stopped.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.12.2 Refuelling will be deemed to have commenced when the fuel tank of the Machine has been",
      "text": "17.3.12.2 Refuelling will be deemed to have commenced when the fuel tank of the Machine has been \nopened and completed when the tank is closed.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.12.3 Smoking is strictly prohibited in areas where refuelling is permitted.",
      "text": "17.3.12.3 Smoking is strictly prohibited in areas where refuelling is permitted.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.12.4 Riders are liable for exclusion from an Event for failing to adhere to GCR 17.3.12, and are",
      "text": "17.3.12.4 Riders are liable for exclusion from an Event for failing to adhere to GCR 17.3.12, and are \nresponsible for the actions of their mechanics and support team members.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.13 Homologation of Fuel",
      "text": "17.3.13 Homologation of Fuel",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.13.1 Unleaded fuel produced by an oil company for sale in the Australian general transport fuel market",
      "text": "17.3.13.1 Unleaded fuel produced by an oil company for sale in the Australian general transport fuel market \nthrough retail petrol pumps in at least five states does not have to be homologated. For the \navoidance of doubt, this means the fuel must be available for sale on demand from a roadside \nbowser outlet at each of at least five separate service stations in each of at least five Australian \nstates or territories.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.13.2 Organisations seeking homologation of fuel must provide MA with:",
      "text": "17.3.13.2 Organisations seeking homologation of fuel must provide MA with:\na)    Two one-litre sealed containers of the fuel for analysis,\nb)    Details of the fuel characteristics,\nc)    The distribution network,\nd)    The price structure,\ne)    A homologation fee of $3,500 in the first year and $2,000 per year thereafter.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.3.13.3 Fuels approved under this GCR will be published at www.ma.org.au.",
      "text": "17.3.13.3 Fuels approved under this GCR will be published at www.ma.org.au.\nIt’s your sport! We welcome your input.\nRefer to the Rule Change Process page on the MA website\nwww.ma.org.au\n\n273\nAPP D: ELECTRIC MOTORCYCLES\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4 APPENDIX D: ELECTRIC MOTORCYCLES",
      "text": "17.4  APPENDIX D: ELECTRIC MOTORCYCLES",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.1 Technical Concept",
      "text": "17.4.1 Technical Concept",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.1.1 The technical concept is reserved for Motorcycles powered solely by stored electricity (battery/",
      "text": "17.4.1.1 The technical concept is reserved for Motorcycles powered solely by stored electricity (battery/\naccumulator) and by the action of the wheel/s in contact with the ground.\na)    For two and/or three-wheeled electric propelled Machines, powered solely by stored \nelectricity (battery/accumulator). The number of electric motors is limited to one for two-\nwheeled and two for three-wheeled Machines.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.2 Approval of Machines",
      "text": "17.4.2 Approval of Machines",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.2.1 All Machines must be approved by MA and amendments to these technical regulations may be",
      "text": "17.4.2.1 All Machines must be approved by MA and amendments to these technical regulations may be \nmade at any time in order to ensure fair competition. For a list of eligible electric Machines, the \nclass and disciplines in which they are eligible to compete, and other specific electric Machine \ninformation, refer to www.ma.org.au.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3 Class Performance Limits",
      "text": "17.4.3 Class Performance Limits",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.1 For MX, SX, Enduro, Dirt Track, Track, Flat Track, Supermoto & Minikhana:",
      "text": "17.4.3.1 For MX, SX, Enduro, Dirt Track, Track, Flat Track, Supermoto & Minikhana:\na)    50cc Auto and Demo (Non-Competitive) class equivalent – An electric motor with a \nmaximum output of up to 4kw, measured at the rear wheel,\nb)    65cc class equivalent – An electric motor with a maximum output of up to 6kw 11kw, \nmeasured at the rear wheel,\nc)    85cc class equivalent – An electric motor with a maximum output of up to 18kw, measured \nat the rear wheel,\nd)    MX2 class equivalent – An electric motor with a maximum output of up to 48hp/36kw, \nmeasured at the motor,\ne)    MX1 class equivalent – An electric motor with a maximum output of up to 60hp/45kw, \nmeasured at the motor, \nf)    E3 and Open class equivalent - An electric motor with a maximum output of up to \n80hp/60kw, measured at the motor.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.2 Switching to a higher power mode above the permitted limit for the class entered during",
      "text": "17.4.3.2 Switching to a higher power mode above the permitted limit for the class entered during \nCompetition is strictly prohibited. Switching to a lower power mode under the limit permitted for \nthe class entered during Competition is allowed.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.3 The power output must be confirmed during technical inspection and may be checked post-Race",
      "text": "17.4.3.3 The power output must be confirmed during technical inspection and may be checked post-Race \nat the designated parc fermé (e.g. a sound test area or podium). Approved methods must be \nused for technical inspections.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.4 Only off road specific Machines used to compete in the listed disciplines above are permitted to",
      "text": "17.4.3.4 Only off road specific Machines used to compete in the listed disciplines above are permitted to \ncompete in the appropriate classes for that discipline.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.5 Road biased Machines are not eligible.",
      "text": "17.4.3.5 Road biased Machines are not eligible.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.6 Discipline specific wheel sizes apply to junior classes utilising these electric Machines.",
      "text": "17.4.3.6 Discipline specific wheel sizes apply to junior classes utilising these electric Machines.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.3.7 Electric Machines are permitted to compete in the appropriate class as listed in the Electric",
      "text": "17.4.3.7 Electric Machines are permitted to compete in the appropriate class as listed in the Electric \nMotorcycle Approved Competition List available at www.ma.org.au at off road Events. In addition \nto these regulations, all Machines must meet Australian Standards for Electric Vehicles and pass \ntechnical inspection prior to being permitted to compete. Refer to 17.4.5 m) i) to vi) for information \non the required standards.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.4 Class Performance Limits (Trial Machines)",
      "text": "17.4.4  Class Performance Limits (Trial Machines)",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.4.1 Power bus maximum voltage:",
      "text": "17.4.4.1 Power bus maximum voltage:\na)    For Trial 1 to 5: The maximum allowed voltage in the main power bus is 120 VDC or 85 \nVAC. A secondary power bus may be used to supply energy to the critical systems at \na maximum voltage of 20 VDC or 14 VAC. For Trial 1 to 5 (300cc equivalent), electric \nMachines up to 22kw are accepted.\nb)    For Trial (125cc equivalent): The maximum allowed voltage in the main power bus is 60 \nVDC (and 30 VAC RMS). A secondary power bus may be used to supply energy to the \ncritical systems at a maximum voltage of 20 VDC or 14 VAC. For 125cc equivalent classes, \nelectric Machines up to 11kw are accepted.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.4.2 Switching to a higher power mode above the permitted limit for the class entered during",
      "text": "17.4.4.2 Switching to a higher power mode above the permitted limit for the class entered during \nCompetition is strictly prohibited. Switching to a lower power mode under the limit permitted for \nthe class entered during Competition is allowed.\n\n274\nAPP D: ELECTRIC MOTORCYCLES\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5 All Disciplines - Technical Requirements:",
      "text": "17.4.5  All Disciplines - Technical Requirements:",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.1 The charging system must be as supplied by the OEM, separate from the Machine, and comply",
      "text": "17.4.5.1 The charging system must be as supplied by the OEM, separate from the Machine, and comply \nwith all Australian and state government electrical safety requirements including thermal overload \ntrip, fusing and be equipped with an earth leakage protection breaker. The charging of Machines \nmust be carried out in accordance with the manufacturers’ instructions.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.2 Charging with OEM wall socket charger up to 3.5Kw/h may be done following the guidelines of",
      "text": "17.4.5.2 Charging with OEM wall socket charger up to 3.5Kw/h may be done following the guidelines of \nthe manufacturer in the general paddock area. The charging system must be automatic and must \nensure that the battery cannot be overcharged or damaged if left permanently connected to the \ncharger.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.3 For fast charging of removable batteries:",
      "text": "17.4.5.3 For fast charging of removable batteries:\na)    A separate fast charging area should be provided and controlled to allow safe fast charging \nof electric Machines or removable batteries. It is not permitted to fast charge Machines in \nthe general paddock area.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.4 A Technical Officer shall check the Machine for compliance with the technical specifications.",
      "text": "17.4.5.4 A Technical Officer shall check the Machine for compliance with the technical specifications.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.5 All Machines must comply in every respect with all the requirements for racing as specified in",
      "text": "17.4.5.5 All Machines must comply in every respect with all the requirements for racing as specified in \nthese GCRs’ technical specifications for each eligible discipline, unless otherwise specified below.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.6 For the accumulator (storage battery):",
      "text": "17.4.5.6 For the accumulator (storage battery):\na)    The accumulator is defined as any equipment used for the intermediate storage of electrical \nenergy supplied by the charging unit. Any on-board accumulator is considered as an \nintegral part of the vehicle’s accumulator,\nb)    The type, dimensions and weight of accumulator/s cannot be changed after Competiton has \ncommenced,\nc)    All on-board electrical equipment, unless consisting of items originally powered by dry \nbatteries, small accumulators or their own solar cells, must receive its energy supply from \nthe machine’s official accumulators.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.7 Recovering energy generated by the kinetic energy of the vehicle is permitted.",
      "text": "17.4.5.7 Recovering energy generated by the kinetic energy of the vehicle is permitted.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.8 The use of any carbon based source of energy in any form whatsoever with the aim of improving",
      "text": "17.4.5.8 The use of any carbon based source of energy in any form whatsoever with the aim of improving \nthe performance of the Machine is strictly prohibited. This includes the energy used to drive the \nMachine’s cooling system.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.9 The Machine must be able to freewheel in the event that the propulsion system has stopped (e.g.",
      "text": "17.4.5.9 The Machine must be able to freewheel in the event that the propulsion system has stopped (e.g. \ncharge exhausted or system failure).",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.10 The voltage is limited to 500 volts nominal between two points (when charging higher charging",
      "text": "17.4.5.10 The voltage is limited to 500 volts nominal between two points (when charging higher charging \nvoltages are permitted).",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.11 A ‘self-closing’ throttle (power control) must be applied.",
      "text": "17.4.5.11 A ‘self-closing’ throttle (power control) must be applied.",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.12 An emergency circuit breaker (kill switch) that isolates the battery/ies and prevents drive must",
      "text": "17.4.5.12 An emergency circuit breaker (kill switch) that isolates the battery/ies and prevents drive must \nbe fitted. The breaker must be fitted in such a position that it can be operated by the rider while \nseated and by an official attending the Machine:\na)    This breaker must be clearly identified as such,\nb)    The use of a lanyard attached to the rider to operate this breaker as an alternative to a \nbutton or switch is recommended for some disciplines (e.g. Dirt Track, Track & Flat Track).",
      "chapter": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": "17.4.5.13 For IEC/AS publications and guidelines:",
      "text": "17.4.5.13 For IEC/AS publications and guidelines:\na)    If no specific rule exists in these GCRs, the relevant IEC/AS Standard (International Electro-\nTechnical Commission Standard or Australian & State electrical safety requirements) or \nrespective report/s should be observed,\nb)    IEC 60529/AS 60529 Degrees of protection provided by enclosures (IP Code),\nc)    IEC 60783/SA TR IEC 60783:2014 Wiring and connectors for the road vehicles. This report \nis applicable to cabling and connectors used in battery electric road vehicles,\nd)    IEC 60784 Instruments for electric road vehicles. This report is applicable to the \ninstrumentation of electric road vehicles, excluding those items which are used as \ninstrumentation in vehicles with internal combustion engines,\ne)    IEC 60785 Rotating Machines for electric road vehicles. This report is applicable to rotating \nelectrical Machines (traction motors and auxiliary motors) of electric road vehicles including \nhybrids, which are fed from the main traction batteries,\n\n275\nAPP D: ELECTRIC MOTORCYCLES\n2026 MANUAL OF MOTORCYCLE SPORT\nf)    IEC 60786 Controllers for electric road vehicles. This report is applicable to the equipment \non electric vehicles that control the rate of energy transfer between the traction battery or \nbatteries and the motor or motors.\n Note: For a list of eligible electric Machines, the class and disciplines in which they are eligible to \ncompete and other specific electric Machine information, is available at www.ma.org.au.",
      "chapter": 17
